Handling HTTP status codes and exceptions properly is crucial for a production-grade API. It ensures your client (the frontend or another service) knows exactly what happened—whether the data was found, if there was a server error, or if the request was invalid.
Here is the professional way to handle this using a Global Exception Handler and proper ResponseEntity construction.
1. Handling Success Status Codes (Controller Layer)
In your DifferenceController, you are currently using ResponseEntity.ok() which sends a 200 OK. However, sometimes you want to handle cases where the search returns no results.
Update your Controller to return 204 No Content if the list is empty, or 200 OK if data is found.
// Inside DifferenceController.java

@PostMapping("/filter/detailed")
public ResponseEntity<Page<DifferenceDTO>> getDifferencesDetailed(
        @RequestBody DifferenceSearchCriteria criteria,
        @RequestParam(defaultValue = "0") int page,
        @RequestParam(defaultValue = "100") int size
) {
    Page<DifferenceDTO> result = service.getDifferencesByComposite(
        criteria.getReconRunDate(),
        criteria.getBranchCode(),
        criteria.getCurrency(),
        criteria.getCgl(),
        page,
        size
    );

    if (result.isEmpty()) {
        // Return 204 No Content (Successful request, but no data matches filters)
        return ResponseEntity.noContent().build();
    }

    // Return 200 OK with the data
    return ResponseEntity.ok(result);
}

2. Handling Exceptions Globally (Global Exception Handler)
Instead of writing try-catch blocks in every controller method, Spring Boot provides @ControllerAdvice. This catches errors across your entire application and returns a clean JSON error response.
Step 2a: Create an Error Response DTO
This ensures your API always returns errors in a consistent format.
package com.fincore.ReportService.dto;

import lombok.AllArgsConstructor;
import lombok.Data;
import java.time.LocalDateTime;

@Data
@AllArgsConstructor
public class ErrorResponse {
    private int statusCode;
    private String message;
    private LocalDateTime timestamp;
    private String details;
}

Step 2b: Create the Global Exception Handler
Create a new class GlobalExceptionHandler.java in a exception package.
package com.fincore.ReportService.exception;

import com.fincore.ReportService.dto.ErrorResponse;
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.ControllerAdvice;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.ExceptionHandler;
import org.springframework.web.context.request.WebRequest;

@ControllerAdvice
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {

    // 1. Handle Specific Custom Exception (e.g., Data Not Found)
    // You can throw this manually in your Service layer if needed
    @ExceptionHandler(IllegalArgumentException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ErrorResponse> handleInvalidArguments(IllegalArgumentException ex, WebRequest request) {
        ErrorResponse error = new ErrorResponse(
            HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST.value(),
            ex.getMessage(),
            LocalDateTime.now(),
            request.getDescription(false)
        );
        return new ResponseEntity<>(error, HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST);
    }

    // 2. Handle General / Unexpected System Errors
    @ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ErrorResponse> handleGlobalException(Exception ex, WebRequest request) {
        ErrorResponse error = new ErrorResponse(
            HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR.value(),
            "An unexpected error occurred. Please contact support.",
            LocalDateTime.now(),
            request.getDescription(false)
        );
        return new ResponseEntity<>(error, HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR);
    }
}

3. How to Trigger These Errors (Validation)
To make this robust, you should validate the inputs. For example, if a user sends a null date, you might want to stop the request immediately.
Add spring-boot-starter-validation to your pom.xml if you haven't already, then use @Valid in your controller.
Updated DTO with Validation:
// DifferenceSearchCriteria.java
import jakarta.validation.constraints.NotNull;

public class DifferenceSearchCriteria {
    @NotNull(message = "Recon Run Date cannot be null")
    private LocalDate reconRunDate;
    // ... other fields
}

Updated Controller with Validation:
// DifferenceController.java
import jakarta.validation.Valid;

@PostMapping("/filter/detailed")
public ResponseEntity<Page<DifferenceDTO>> getDifferencesDetailed(
        @Valid @RequestBody DifferenceSearchCriteria criteria, // <--- Add @Valid here
        @RequestParam(defaultValue = "0") int page,
        @RequestParam(defaultValue = "100") int size
) {
    // ... logic
}

Summary of Result
 * If data is found: Returns 200 OK with JSON list.
 * If no data is found: Returns 204 No Content (Empty body).
 * If user sends bad data (e.g. null date): Returns 400 Bad Request with your custom JSON error message.
 * If server crashes (DB down): Returns 500 Internal Server Error with a safe message (hiding internal stack traces).
